WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.900
Today you will learn four simple parts that make AI replies useful the first time.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.550
Role tells the AI who to be, for example a polite customer-care helper for your shop.

3
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:16.880
Task says exactly what you want, for example a WhatsApp reply to a customer.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.270
Start every prompt you write with these two simple parts.

5
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:29.480
Context is the background the AI cannot guess: who the reader is, what happened, and the facts.

6
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.770
The AI does not know your shop, your school or your client.

7
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:38.580
The more useful facts you give, the fewer facts it makes up.

8
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.920
Format is the shape of the answer: length, bullet points, a table, Hindi or English, formal or friendly.

9
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.810
Saying “3 short lines for WhatsApp” saves you from editing a long essay.

10
00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:53.220
The first reply is a draft, not the end.

11
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:58.230
Reply with “shorter”, “more polite” or “in Hindi” to improve it.

12
00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:03.200
For bigger tasks, add “Ask me any questions you need before you write.”

13
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.470
Here is the prompt most people type first: write a reply to a customer.

14
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:14.040
The answer is polite, but it says nothing, because the AI had to guess everything.

15
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.900
Now the same job with all four parts: role, task, context and format.

16
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:27.470
The reply has the real reason, the new day and time, and fits on a phone screen.

17
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:33.580
Same tool, same minute, but a far better answer, because you gave it what it needed.

18
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.340
For bigger tasks, ask the AI to ask you questions first.

19
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:44.950
Gemini asks for the date, the classes and what to bring, which are things a good assistant would check.

20
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:50.260
You answer in one message, and the notice comes out right the first time.

21
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.360
Translation is a daily job, so let’s try a customer’s message in Hinglish.

22
00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:02.170
Copilot says the payment was sent yesterday, but the customer said they will send it by tomorrow.

23
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:08.230
In Hindi, kal means both yesterday and tomorrow, and the AI guessed wrong.

24
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:13.660
If you can read both languages, always check the meaning, not just the grammar.

25
00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.150
Good prompts are worth saving, just like a good letter format.

26
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:24.840
Put brackets around the parts that change, and you can reuse a prompt in seconds, in any tool.

27
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.160
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

28
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.410
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
